  <section class="plate plate--sunken">
    <div class="shell plate__inner">
      <div class="plate__head">
        <p class="plate__num">§ 07</p>
        <h2>Where it actually is</h2>
        <p class="plate__sub">
          Fern is pre-1.0 and the nightly build is the release channel. Syntax
          still moves. Here is the honest state of each part, and the
          <a href={href("status/")}>status page</a> keeps the longer version.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="status">
        <div class="status__row">
          <span class="status__state" data-state="solid">Solid</span>
          <span class="status__what">Language &amp; type system</span>
          <span class="status__note"
            >Generics, traits, <code>dyn</code>, sum types, exhaustive matching,
            errors as values, ownership modes. Associated types are native-only
            so far.</span
          >
        </div>
        <div class="status__row">
          <span class="status__state" data-state="solid">Solid</span>
          <span class="status__what">Native targets</span>
          <span class="status__note"
            >arm64 Linux, macOS and Android, and x86-64 Linux, all assembled and
            linked in-process. x86-64 is the newer of the two and has a few
            gaps.</span
          >
        </div>
        <div class="status__row">
          <span class="status__state" data-state="solid">Solid</span>
          <span class="status__what">WebAssembly</span>
          <span class="status__note"
            >Preview 2 components composed natively. Coverage and the sanitizer
            are native-only; heap exhaustion traps rather than exiting
            cleanly.</span
          >
        </div>
        <div class="status__row">
          <span class="status__state" data-state="moving">Moving</span>
          <span class="status__what">Reference counting</span>
          <span class="status__note"
            >Complete and shipping on the Go implementation; the port to the
            self-hosted compiler is the project's live front. Some shapes still
            retain memory — the leak census is pinned per fixture rather than
            waved at.</span
          >
        </div>
        <div class="status__row">
          <span class="status__state" data-state="moving">Moving</span>
          <span class="status__what">Self-hosting</span>
          <span class="status__note"
            >The compiler builds itself and bootstraps with no Go present. The
            remaining gate is memory: the self-host compiler frees less than the
            Go one, and the second bootstrap stage does not yet fit in a
            reasonable heap.</span
          >
        </div>
        <div class="status__row">
          <span class="status__state" data-state="moving">Moving</span>
          <span class="status__what">Package capabilities</span>
          <span class="status__note"
            >Grants are enforced with the full call chain in the error, but a
            dependency that declares nothing is still allowed with a warning.
            Default-deny is the next step, so do not treat it as a sandbox
            yet.</span
          >
        </div>
        <div class="status__row">
          <span class="status__state" data-state="absent">Not yet</span>
          <span class="status__what">Threads &amp; parallelism</span>
          <span class="status__note"
            >Concurrency is I/O-driven futures on one poll loop. Reference
            counts are non-atomic by design, so there are no threads and no
            multicore.</span
          >
        </div>
        <div class="status__row">
          <span class="status__state" data-state="absent">Not yet</span>
          <span class="status__what">Bare metal</span>
          <span class="status__note"
            >The freestanding targets type-check against an empty capability set
            and no backend emits for them yet. The blocker is not codegen: an
            interrupt handler is a second context racing non-atomic
            refcounts.</span
          >
        </div>
        <div class="status__row">
          <span class="status__state" data-state="absent">Not yet</span>
          <span class="status__what">Windows, Intel Macs, 32-bit</span>
          <span class="status__note"
            >None are planned. If you need one of them, one of the languages on
            the <a href={href("why/")}>Why Fern</a> page is the honest answer.</span
          >
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
